CS 214 Fall 2026 - Project I: My little malloc()

Names: Jacob Blunt and Pranav Srivats  
NetIDs: jab1062, ps1398

Test Plan:

We wanted to test:

1. If allocated pointers have 8-byte alignment. We checked the spacing between pointers and stored a double.
2. If allocated objects overlap and overwrite each other. We filled different objects with different values and checked that their data stayed unchanged.
3. If freed memory can be used again. We freed an object and allocated another object of the same size to check that the space was reused.
4. If adjacent free chunks are combined correctly. We freed neighboring objects and allocated a larger object that needed their combined space.
5. If errors are detected. We tested outside pointers, interior pointers, double frees, and allocation failure.
6. If leaked memory is detected. We deliberately left an object allocated and checked the leak message.

Test Programs:

The programs we used to test:

test_accuracy: Tests alignment, data preservation, reuse, and coalescing. Takes no arguments.

test_mistakes: Takes one test number:
1. Outside pointer
2. Interior pointer
3. Double free
4. Allocation failure
5. Memory leak

Cases 1–3 should exit with status 2. Cases 4–5 should exit with status 0 and print the expected error or leak message.

memtest: Allocates multiple objects, fills them with different values, and checks that their data stays correct. Takes no arguments.

memgrind: Runs five allocation and free workloads 50 times and reports the average execution time. Takes no arguments.

Design Notes:

The heap is a 4096-byte array divided into chunks. Every chunk has an 8-byte header containing its size and whether it is allocated.

mymalloc() rounds requested sizes up to multiples of 8 and searches for the first free chunk that is large enough.

myfree() marks a chunk as free and combines adjacent free chunks.

The heap is initialized automatically when mymalloc() or myfree() is first called.
